scripts/sloshing/sph.py

  This file is a modified version of the Smoothed Particle Hydrodynamics example
  distributed with NVIDIA Warp (https://github.com/NVIDIA/warp).

      Copyright (c) 2022 NVIDIA CORPORATION & AFFILIATES. All rights reserved.
      SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0

  The original copyright and licence header is retained at the top of the file.
  It was branched on 8 October 2025 and modified by Samuel Seligardi between
  October and November 2025. As required by section 4(b) of the Apache License,
  the modifications are listed here:

    - all simulation parameters converted to the CGS unit system (cm, g, s),
      with unit annotations throughout
    - particle mass derived from the reference density and particle spacing
      (m = rho * dp^3) instead of being set independently
    - timestep derived from the target frame rate and the number of substeps
      per frame (step_dt = 1 / (fps * substeps))
    - rectangular boundaries replaced by a container with slanted walls,
      parameterised by wall slope on the x and z axes
    - container boundaries made translatable, so the tank can be moved during
      the simulation
    - prescribed container motion profile added (settling, acceleration,
      constant velocity, braking, rest phases)
    - hash grid cell size and bucket count derived explicitly from the
      smoothing length and the domain volume, with the reasoning documented
    - per-frame maximum fluid height tracking and wall-clock timing added
    - docstrings added throughout, in NumPy style

  The underlying method is that of Mueller, Charypar and Gross, "Particle-based
  fluid simulation for interactive applications", Symposium on Computer
  Animation, 2003, as cited in the original example.

